<Project>

  <!--
    The developer-tools switch.

    Copy this file next to it as `Directory.Build.local.props` to compile the developer surfaces
    into your local build. That name is git-ignored and CI fails if it is ever committed, because
    that is the only route by which a published build could carry the tools.

        copy Directory.Build.local.props.example Directory.Build.local.props

    What the symbol adds: a diagnostics window that shows the raw gearset entries as they are
    read, the reconciliation result including which stage matched each record, and the state of
    the cached snapshot.

    What it does not affect: logging. Removing the tools from a release must not remove the
    ability to work out what went wrong on a player's machine, so the log survives and only the
    interactive surface goes. Otherwise a bug report arrives with no evidence attached.

    Verify the claim against the built assembly rather than against the source: build both ways
    and read the type table. The source is what you intended, the artefact is what shipped.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);GEARBOOK_DEVTOOLS</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
